From d9278d233ae4e37fc865c6405ee9651e33f01c69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kitae Noh <5194831+nworkers@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:15:49 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] emit --trampoline: body-ending transfers without host stack

Transfers that end a body (a fall-out into the next function, a `j` to
another function, the taken path of a likely branch, an indirect `jr`, a
`jr $ra` used as a jump) are emitted as C tail calls. Whether those become
jumps is up to the C compiler: unoptimized builds, and some optimized
ones, keep a host frame per transfer, so a guest loop that runs through
several functions grows the host stack without bound.

With --trampoline (off by default; off, the output is unchanged), such a
transfer stores its target in the per-thread psp_next and returns
(PSP_TAIL), and psp_run() calls a function and then the chain it leaves.
Guest calls keep going through psp_call_guest_fn/psp_call_guest_addr,
which now run the callee through psp_run, so the host stack grows only
with guest calls. psp_dispatch runs its target through psp_run,
psp_dispatch_tail is the tail form, a guest unwind (psp_cpu.unwind) ends
a chain, and psp_call_from_host drops anything a stopped run left behind.
--trampoline combines with --base and --hints on the emit command line.

Tests: the emitter fixtures for fall-outs, `j`, likely branches, `jr` and
`bal` with the option on; a 100,000-round cycle through three functions,
one of them indirect, runs one frame deep, both from psp_run and through
psp_call_guest_fn; unwinding and host entry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
